# mggob.msg - Gob, bartender of Moriarty's Saloon (mggob.ssl).
# 100-109 look and examine, 110-119 floating lines, 200+ conversation.
# One entry per line; no braces in comment lines.

{100}{}{You see Gob, the bartender.}
{101}{}{A ghoul in a stained apron, polishing a glass that will never be clean. He watches your hands when you move, the way a dog watches a boot.}

{110}{}{[He thumps the radio. It hisses.]}
{111}{}{Sorry. Sorry.}
{112}{}{Clean enough. It's clean enough.}
{113}{}{No trouble. No trouble at all.}
{114}{}{It's only static. It's always static.}

# First meeting.
{200}{}{[The ghoul flinches before you have said a word.] Sorry. Sorry. Habit. I wasn't staring. What'll it be?}
{201}{}{Easy. I never met a ghoul behind a bar, that's all.}
{202}{}{What happened to your face?}
{203}{}{Just a drink.}
{204}{}{You look like jerky man. Me like jerky.}
{205}{}{Nothing. Never mind.}

{210}{}{[He waits for the rest of it. It doesn't come.] Huh. Most folks lead with the smell. I'm Gob. For you, a tenth off. Don't tell Moriarty. He thinks kindness is a kind of theft.}
{211}{}{The War happened to it. Then a hundred and sixty years. Then customers. Drinks are on the board. So are the prices.}
{212}{}{[A dry, cracking sound. It takes you a moment to place it as laughter.] Jerky man. Fifteen years here, and that's the first thing anyone has said that I'll keep. I'm Gob. You drink cheap.}

# Later meetings: 220 friend, 221 neither, 222 insulted, 223 friend after the bomb is disarmed, 224 Simms dead.
{220}{}{Hey. It's you. Good to see a face that isn't about to yell. What can I get you?}
{221}{}{What'll it be?}
{222}{}{Board's on the wall. Point at something.}
{223}{}{They say you killed the bomb. I lay awake listening for the tick anyway. Fifteen years of habit. What can I get you?}
{224}{}{Sheriff's dead, and Moriarty docked me for the mess on the floor. What'll it be?}

{230}{}{What have you got?}
{231}{}{How did you end up here?}
{232}{}{Is there any way round Moriarty's price?}
{233}{}{He won't hear it from me. Any way round his price?}
{234}{}{The man in the corner. Who is he?}
{235}{}{What became of the man in the suit?}
{236}{}{Is that radio broken?}
{237}{}{Here. A hundred and eighty. Call it back pay.}
{238}{}{Take care of yourself, Gob.}
{239}{}{Drink!}
{240}{}{Bye bye, jerky man.}
{241}{}{I'm leaving.}
{242}{}{About your face. I shouldn't have said that.}
{243}{}{Your wages, out of Moriarty's strongbox. A hundred and eighty.}
{244}{}{Me find Gob shinies in bad man box. Here.}

{250}{}{I come from a town of people like me, a long walk from here. Slavers took me on the road. Moriarty bought me cheap and wrote it down as a debt. Fifteen years I've paid on it. It's bigger now than when I started.}
{251}{}{Does he pay you at all?}
{252}{}{On paper. There's a strongbox in his office with my wages in it, and Nova's. A hundred and eighty, last I heard him count. I've never held a coin of it. Forget I said that. Please.}
{253}{}{It's a long story, and you'd only ask about my face again.}

{260}{}{[He wipes the same glass twice.] There's a terminal in his office. Everything he knows, he types into it. Nova knows the password. You never heard this from me.}
{261}{}{I can't. If he found out, he'd take it out of my hide, and there isn't much hide left. Sorry. Sorry.}

# The man in the suit: 270 still in his corner, 271 left town, 272 dead, 273 shot Simms, 274 waiting outside.
{270}{}{Sits. Watches the door. Tips nothing. He asked me once what it's like, being a monster. I didn't answer. I thought of a good one around closing time.}
{271}{}{Gone. Left a full glass and a tip for the boss, none for me. First time that corner's been empty in a month. I keep wiping it anyway.}
{272}{}{Dead. Fifteen years behind this bar, and that's the first bad news I ever poured a drink to.}
{273}{}{He shot the sheriff right there and walked out like a man leaving church. I was under the bar. I'm always under the bar.}
{274}{}{He paid his tab and walked out. Paid it in full. Men like that only settle up when they don't plan on coming back.}

{280}{}{[He thumps the radio. It hisses at him.] Static. It's always static. Some nights I think the static is improving.}
{281}{}{[Thump.] ...There. Hear that? No. Me neither.}
{282}{}{[He raises his fist, then lowers it.] Let it rest. It's had a hard hundred and sixty years. Haven't we all.}

{290}{}{[He stares at the money as if it might bite.] That's... nobody ever... I'll hide Nova's half where he never looks. Under the good whiskey. Thank you. I mean that.}
{291}{}{Anything else?}
{292}{}{One more thing.}
{293}{}{More drink!}
{294}{}{Point at what you want. No, that's the radio. That's me. There you go.}
{295}{}{Anything else? Take your time. He isn't watching.}
{296}{}{What.}
{297}{}{[He stops wiping.] ...Huh. People say it all the time. Nobody takes it back. All right. Regular prices. Don't make me sorry.}
